      "local_ref": "d1",
      "document_summary": "The paper by A. G. Vlachopoulos examines the 
so-called Ring of Nestor (CMS VI, no. 277) with the aim of documenting its 
authenticity and reassessing earlier scholarly tendencies to treat certain 
Creto-Mycenaean gold rings, including the Rings of Nestor and Minos, as 
forgeries. The study argues that recent discoveries of comparable iconographic 
subjects in Aegean art and detailed analysis of ring-making techniques support a
revision in favor of the rings' authenticity. A key focus is the central 
pictorial motif on the Ring of Nestor, previously described by Arthur Evans as a
watch-dog or dragon guarding a world-tree. Vlachopoulos identifies this motif 
instead as a scorpion, interpreted as a major apotropaic symbol in Bronze Age 
Aegean iconography, which interacts with or \"kills\" the so-called Minoan 
Dragon and thus offers a new interpretation of the ring's complex imagery. The 
text also provides measurements of the ring and situates it among the largest 
known Aegean signet rings from the 16th–15th centuries BC.",
      "source_ref": "s1"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d2",
      "document_summary": "The paper \"Some Observations on the Iconography of 
the ‘Ring of Nestor’\" analyzes the imagery on the Ring of Nestor within the 
broader context of debates about Aegean forgeries and authenticity. It revisits 
key iconographic features, including the depiction of incurved altars, and 
compares them with other material to assess the ring's significance in Minoan 
and Mycenaean art and its possible authenticity. The author emphasizes 
contradictions or limits in technical analyses and argues for the importance of 
iconographic study in evaluating such objects. The paper also notes 
long-standing scholarly disputes over the genuineness of both the Rings of 
Nestor and Minos and advocates against the a priori dismissal of large corpora 
of unprovenanced or recently discovered rings, suggesting that such material can
be an important resource for understanding ancient societies and ring 
typology.",
