"20260627192743267303_d5824bec", "type": "Document", "source_id": 
"20260627192743180682_a0a2f1ea", "text": "The paper by A. G. Vlachopoulos 
examines the so-called Ring of Nestor (CMS VI, no. 277) with the aim of 
documenting its authenticity and reassessing earlier scholarly tendencies to 
treat certain Creto-Mycenaean gold rings, including the Rings of Nestor and 
Minos, as forgeries. The study argues that recent discoveries of comparable 
iconographic subjects in Aegean art and detailed analysis of ring-making 
techniques support a revision in favor of the rings' authenticity. A key focus 
is the central pictorial motif on the Ring of Nestor, previously described by 
Arthur Evans as a watch-dog or dragon guarding a world-tree. Vlachopoulos 
identifies this motif instead as a scorpion, interpreted as a major apotropaic 
symbol in Bronze Age Aegean iconography, which interacts with or \"kills\" the 
so-called Minoan Dragon and thus offers a new interpretation of the ring's 
complex imagery. The text also provides measurements of the ring and situates it
among the largest known Aegean signet rings from the 16th–15th centuries BC."}, 
