“Ring of Nestor” academic article PDF                                           

 • “The many years of research that led to the present paper on the so-called   
   Ring of Nestor (CMS VI, nο. 277), with the documentation of its authenticity,
   have offered me the opportunity to discuss the cases of erroneous            
   identifications of some Creto-Mycenaean gold rings as fakes… This revision   
   has been significantly reinforced by recent finds of iconographic subjects of
   comparable resonance (wall-paintings, gold rings, figurines, ivories, and so 
   on), as well as by more thorough study of the techniques and craftsmanship of
   these unique signet rings of the first centuries of the Late Bronze Age      
   (16th-15th century BC).” [1]                                                 
 • “The key pictorial unit of the Ring of Nestor examined in this paper is      
   Evans’s ‘watch-dog’ or ‘dragon’, namely the guardian of the old ‘tree of the 
   world’, whose branches host the most populous scene in Aegean glyptic art.   
   This motif is here identified as a scorpion, a major apotropaic symbol in    
   Bronze Αge Aegean iconography, which kills the ‘Μinoan Dragon’ of the ring   
   and offers a sound interpretation of the ring’s iconography.” [1]            
 • “The Ring of Nestor, measuring 3.29 cm by 2.18 cm, is the fifth largest      
   Aegean signet ring.” [1]                                                     
 • “This paper presents an analysis of the iconography surrounding the 'Ring of 
   Nestor' in the context of Aegean forgeries and authenticity debates. By      
   revisiting key iconographic features and comparisons, the paper aims to      
   contribute to the ongoing discourse regarding the ring's significance in     
   Minoan and Mycenaean art.” [2]                                               
 • “The connection between incurved altars and the ring's depiction supports its
   authenticity.” [2]                                                           
 • “Generations of scholars have debated the genuineness of both Nestor's and   
   Minos' rings.” [2]                                                           
 • “Abstract. In the past the authenticity of the so-called Ring of Nestor has  
   been debated mostly in terms of iconography. Arguments against its ancient …”
   [3]                                                                          
 • "The 'Ring of Nestor': A Glimpse into the Minoan After-World." Journal of    
   Hellenic Studies 45: 43-75. [4]                                              
 • Modern scholarship includes dedicated academic studies that critically       
   examine the Ring of Nestor’s iconography and authenticity within the broader 
   context of Aegean forgeries and Creto‑Mycenaean gold rings. [2]              
 • The Ring of Nestor is catalogued as CMS VI, no. 277 and is described as the  
   fifth largest known Aegean signet ring, measuring approximately 3.29 cm by   
   2.18 cm. [1]                                                                 
 • Vlachopoulos argues, on the basis of iconographic comparisons and technical  
   studies of Creto‑Mycenaean gold rings, that the Ring of Nestor (CMS VI, no.  
   277) is an authentic Late Bronze Age artifact, thereby overturning earlier   
   1920s claims that it was a modern forgery. [1]                               
 • The authenticity of the so‑called Ring of Nestor has been the subject of     
   long‑running scholarly debate, with past arguments against its ancient origin
   focusing primarily on iconographic features rather than technical analysis.  
   [2]                                                                          
 • At least one modern study on the Ring of Nestor contends that contradictions 
   and limitations in technical analyses of Aegean artifacts highlight the      
   importance of iconographic study when evaluating authenticity, including for 
   the Ring of Nestor. [2]                                                      
 • One iconographic study argues that the depiction of incurved altars on the   
   Ring of Nestor and their parallels elsewhere in Aegean art support the ring’s
   authenticity. [2]                                                            
 • Vlachopoulos reinterprets the principal pictorial motif on the Ring of       
   Nestor—previously described by Evans as a watch‑dog or dragon guarding a     
   world‑tree—as a scorpion functioning as a major apotropaic symbol in Bronze  
   Age Aegean iconography and symbolically killing the ‘Minoan Dragon’ of the   
   ring. [1]                                                                    
 • A recent study describes how new finds of related iconographic subjects (such
   as wall paintings, gold rings, figurines, and ivories) and closer study of   
   ring‑making techniques and craftsmanship have strengthened the case for the  
   authenticity of the Ring of Nestor and similar Creto‑Mycenaean signet rings  
   from the early Late Bronze Age. [1]                                          
 • One paper warns against the a priori exclusion of unprovenanced or poorly    
   documented ancient and medieval finger rings, including the Ring of Nestor,  
   as potential sources of data for understanding past societies and material   
   culture. [2]                                                                 
 • The study titled "The Pseudo‑Minoan Nestor Ring and Its Egyptian Iconography"
   treats a ring associated with Nestor that is characterized as pseudo‑Minoan  
   and analyzes its imagery in relation to Egyptian motifs, engaging with       
   earlier interpretations such as Evans’s article "The 'Ring of Nestor': A     
   Glimpse into the Minoan After‑World" and Frazer’s "The Golden Bough". [4]    

